1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,735
Barings, 1995: The Singapore Trading

2
00:00:03,815 --> 00:00:07,525
Desk That Broke a 233-Year-Old Bank

3
00:00:07,525 --> 00:00:11,694
On the afternoon of Thursday
the 23rd of February 1995,

4
00:00:11,774 --> 00:00:16,328
Nick Leeson told a colleague
at Barings' office in Singapore

5
00:00:16,408 --> 00:00:20,425
that he had to visit his
wife in hospital, and left.

6
00:00:20,425 --> 00:00:22,350
He did not come back.

7
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:25,794
Leeson ran the bank's futures
business on the Singapore

8
00:00:25,874 --> 00:00:28,869
International Monetary
Exchange, known as SIMEX,

9
00:00:28,949 --> 00:00:32,649
and over two and a half years
he had hidden mounting losses

10
00:00:32,729 --> 00:00:36,125
in an account numbered eight
eight eight eight eight.

11
00:00:36,125 --> 00:00:38,411
Three days later Barings,

12
00:00:38,491 --> 00:00:42,750
a London bank founded
in 1762, was insolvent.

13
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:47,179
Barings was founded in
1762 by Francis Baring,

14
00:00:47,259 --> 00:00:52,650
and by 1995 it was the oldest
merchant bank in Britain.

15
00:00:52,650 --> 00:00:56,075
It had come close to failing once before.

16
00:00:56,075 --> 00:01:01,022
In 1890, heavy losses on Argentine
debt threatened its solvency,

17
00:01:01,102 --> 00:01:05,500
and a consortium of banks was
put together to rescue it.

18
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:09,775
A century later it was a
respected but old-fashioned house,

19
00:01:09,855 --> 00:01:14,875
still run from its head office on
Bishopsgate in the City of London.

20
00:01:14,875 --> 00:01:20,085
Barings' futures arm in Singapore,
Baring Futures (Singapore),

21
00:01:20,165 --> 00:01:22,725
had been incorporated in 1986.

22
00:01:22,725 --> 00:01:25,621
It was a small unit, and for most of its

23
00:01:25,701 --> 00:01:29,050
life it attracted little
attention in London.

24
00:01:29,050 --> 00:01:32,715
Leeson had joined Barings
in London in 1989 as a

25
00:01:32,795 --> 00:01:36,772
clerk in its settlements
department, the office that

26
00:01:36,852 --> 00:01:40,675
processes and records
trades after they are made.

27
00:01:40,675 --> 00:01:46,980
He arrived in Singapore in April 1992 to
manage the futures operation's paperwork,

28
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:50,562
and after passing the
Institute of Banking and

29
00:01:50,642 --> 00:01:55,625
Finance's futures trading test
in June he began trading as well.

30
00:01:55,625 --> 00:02:00,852
In July 1992 Baring Futures
(Singapore) started trading on SIMEX,

31
00:02:00,932 --> 00:02:05,342
whose trading floor was in
OUB Centre at Raffles Place,

32
00:02:05,422 --> 00:02:08,525
with Leeson as one of its two traders.

33
00:02:08,525 --> 00:02:12,369
On the 3rd of July 1992 he
opened an error account,

34
00:02:12,449 --> 00:02:15,450
numbered eight eight eight eight eight.

35
00:02:15,450 --> 00:02:19,082
Error accounts are a normal
part of a trading business,

36
00:02:19,162 --> 00:02:22,052
used to park small
mistakes, such as a trade

37
00:02:22,132 --> 00:02:25,575
done at the wrong price,
until they are sorted out.

38
00:02:25,575 --> 00:02:28,098
Leeson used this one to hide the

39
00:02:28,178 --> 00:02:31,350
losses on his own unauthorised trading.

40
00:02:31,350 --> 00:02:35,622
By the end of September 1992,
according to the inspectors

41
00:02:35,702 --> 00:02:39,593
later appointed by Singapore's
Minister for Finance,

42
00:02:39,673 --> 00:02:44,025
the account held losses of
8.8 million Singapore dollars.

43
00:02:44,025 --> 00:02:47,399
In June 1993 Leeson was made general

44
00:02:47,479 --> 00:02:51,125
manager of Baring Futures (Singapore).

45
00:02:51,125 --> 00:02:55,148
He was in charge of the front
office, which did the trading,

46
00:02:55,228 --> 00:02:59,400
and also of the back office,
which processed and recorded it.

47
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:01,682
The person placing the trades was

48
00:03:01,762 --> 00:03:04,625
therefore the same person checking them.

49
00:03:04,625 --> 00:03:09,178
An internal audit in July and
August 1994 warned that this was

50
00:03:09,258 --> 00:03:13,811
a serious risk, but the bank
kept Leeson in both roles because

51
00:03:13,891 --> 00:03:18,300
of his experience and the
profits he appeared to be making.

52
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:22,650
Those reported profits had
made him a star in London.

53
00:03:22,650 --> 00:03:27,824
By the end of December 1994, the
losses hidden in account eight

54
00:03:27,904 --> 00:03:33,575
eight eight eight eight had reached
373.9 million Singapore dollars.

55
00:03:33,575 --> 00:03:36,930
In January 1995 a senior auditor found

56
00:03:37,010 --> 00:03:40,625
a discrepancy in the Singapore accounts.

57
00:03:40,625 --> 00:03:44,120
Leeson explained it with
a story about a trade with

58
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:46,994
an American firm, Spear, Leeds & Kellogg,

59
00:03:47,074 --> 00:03:51,000
and produced a doctored payment
statement to support it.

60
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,689
In the same month, SIMEX
alerted Barings to the size

61
00:03:54,769 --> 00:03:58,096
of its positions in Nikkei
futures and options,

62
00:03:58,176 --> 00:04:01,800
the contracts that track
Japan's main stock index.

63
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:04,906
Barings' senior management told SIMEX that

64
00:04:04,986 --> 00:04:08,475
the bank had enough
assets to back the trades.

65
00:04:08,475 --> 00:04:13,393
Leeson's positions in
early 1995 depended on the

66
00:04:13,473 --> 00:04:19,200
Nikkei 225 index not falling
below about 19,000 points.

67
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:24,198
At 5.46 in the morning on
the 17th of January 1995,

68
00:04:24,278 --> 00:04:27,583
a powerful earthquake struck Kobe,

69
00:04:27,663 --> 00:04:30,750
killing more than 6,400 people.

70
00:04:30,750 --> 00:04:35,016
Japanese shares fell, and by the 23rd of

71
00:04:35,096 --> 00:04:39,225
January the Nikkei was down to 17,785.

72
00:04:39,225 --> 00:04:42,517
Instead of cutting his
losses, Leeson bought more,

73
00:04:42,597 --> 00:04:45,025
hoping the market would bounce back.

74
00:04:45,025 --> 00:04:48,973
It kept sliding, and on
the 23rd of February,

75
00:04:49,053 --> 00:04:51,659
when the index touched 17,580,

76
00:04:51,739 --> 00:04:56,125
his losses reached 1.4
billion Singapore dollars.

77
00:04:56,125 --> 00:05:00,868
That afternoon, a senior settlements
clerk on secondment from London

78
00:05:00,948 --> 00:05:05,700
noticed the discrepancy in Leeson's
account and asked him about it.

79
00:05:05,700 --> 00:05:08,541
Leeson said he had to go to the hospital,

80
00:05:08,621 --> 00:05:12,325
and that night he and his
wife flew to Kuala Lumpur.

81
00:05:12,325 --> 00:05:17,325
At first his colleagues feared he
had stolen money and disappeared.

82
00:05:17,325 --> 00:05:19,512
On the 24th of February they found

83
00:05:19,592 --> 00:05:22,125
account eight eight eight eight eight.

84
00:05:22,125 --> 00:05:26,075
Barings did not have the
money to cover the losses.

85
00:05:26,075 --> 00:05:30,586
The Bank of England tried to
arrange a rescue and failed,

86
00:05:30,666 --> 00:05:36,787
and on Sunday the 26th of February 1995
Barings was placed in administration,

87
00:05:36,867 --> 00:05:40,975
with total losses of 2.2
billion Singapore dollars.

88
00:05:40,975 --> 00:05:43,860
The next day SIMEX placed Baring Futures

89
00:05:43,940 --> 00:05:47,350
(Singapore) under interim
judicial management.

90
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:51,629
On the 6th of March the
Dutch group ING completed

91
00:05:51,709 --> 00:05:55,542
its takeover of Barings,
paying a nominal £1

92
00:05:55,622 --> 00:05:59,625
and putting in £540
million to pay creditors.

93
00:05:59,625 --> 00:06:03,459
Leeson and his wife went on
from Kuala Lumpur to Sabah,

94
00:06:03,539 --> 00:06:06,662
and on the 1st of March
they took a flight to

95
00:06:06,742 --> 00:06:10,300
Frankfurt by way of Brunei,
Bangkok and Abu Dhabi.

96
00:06:10,300 --> 00:06:12,746
They were detained on arrival at

97
00:06:12,826 --> 00:06:15,825
Frankfurt airport on the 2nd of March.

98
00:06:15,825 --> 00:06:19,796
Leeson first fought Singapore's
request to extradite him,

99
00:06:19,876 --> 00:06:22,426
asking instead to be sent to Britain,

100
00:06:22,506 --> 00:06:26,700
but in October he decided to
return to Singapore for trial.

101
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:31,850
He was extradited on the
23rd of November 1995.

102
00:06:31,850 --> 00:06:37,276
On the 1st of December he pleaded guilty
to two charges of fraud and forgery,

103
00:06:37,356 --> 00:06:41,075
and was sentenced to six
and a half years in prison.

104
00:06:41,075 --> 00:06:44,046
He was released on the 3rd of July

105
00:06:44,126 --> 00:06:47,625
1999 with remission for good behaviour.

106
00:06:47,625 --> 00:06:51,835
The collapse was reported around
the world as a London story,

107
00:06:51,915 --> 00:06:53,944
but the trading, the fraud and

108
00:06:54,024 --> 00:06:57,400
much of the investigation
happened in Singapore.

109
00:06:57,400 --> 00:07:00,676
In early March the
Commercial Affairs Department

110
00:07:00,756 --> 00:07:04,313
began a fraud investigation,
and on the 9th of March

111
00:07:04,393 --> 00:07:06,900
the Minister for Finance, Richard Hu,

112
00:07:06,980 --> 00:07:11,525
appointed two inspectors to look
into Baring Futures (Singapore).

113
00:07:11,525 --> 00:07:15,226
Their report, released on
the 17th of October 1995,

114
00:07:15,306 --> 00:07:22,120
blamed Barings' senior management for failures
that allowed Leeson's trades to go undetected,

115
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:26,425
and found that SIMEX should
have audited the firm sooner.

116
00:07:26,425 --> 00:07:30,795
In June 1996 the Commercial
Affairs Department dropped its

117
00:07:30,875 --> 00:07:36,934
investigation into four other men, including
two of Leeson's former supervisors,

118
00:07:37,014 --> 00:07:40,850
finding insufficient grounds
for criminal charges.

119
00:07:40,850 --> 00:07:44,091
SIMEX itself made no
loss from the collapse,

120
00:07:44,171 --> 00:07:48,775
and no other financial institution
in Singapore was affected.

121
00:07:48,775 --> 00:07:52,751
Even so, the Futures Trading
Act was amended with effect

122
00:07:52,831 --> 00:07:56,879
from the 1st of April 1995,
giving the Monetary Authority

123
00:07:56,959 --> 00:08:00,646
of Singapore closer oversight
of futures traders and

124
00:08:00,726 --> 00:08:04,992
requiring them to be licensed,
and further measures followed

125
00:08:05,072 --> 00:08:09,200
to strengthen the regulation
of futures trading on SIMEX.

126
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:13,047
At the time I was on the
short-term interest rate trading

127
00:08:13,127 --> 00:08:16,629
desk at Security Pacific
National Bank in Singapore,

128
00:08:16,709 --> 00:08:22,002
later part of Bank of America, and we
were trading futures on SIMEX ourselves,

129
00:08:22,082 --> 00:08:24,975
so the scandal was the talk of the market.

130
00:08:24,975 --> 00:08:28,008
In the foreign exchange and money markets,

131
00:08:28,088 --> 00:08:31,275
Barings was not a name
we came across much.

132
00:08:31,275 --> 00:08:35,575
It was known as a top British
credit, and not much more.

133
00:08:35,575 --> 00:08:39,639
When the news broke, it sounded
like a story you hear every so

134
00:08:39,719 --> 00:08:44,452
often in the markets: someone builds
a reputation as a brilliant trader,

135
00:08:44,532 --> 00:08:48,275
and the results turn out to
come from something illegal.

136
00:08:48,275 --> 00:08:51,292
The lesson I took from
it, and never forgot,

137
00:08:51,372 --> 00:08:54,750
is that there is no such
thing as a star trader.

138
00:08:54,750 --> 00:08:58,864
There are people who make money
from speculation consistently,

139
00:08:58,944 --> 00:09:01,570
but they usually do it with a systematic

140
00:09:01,650 --> 00:09:05,100
approach to each day's
trading, not by being stars.

141
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:07,125
Could it happen again?

142
00:09:07,125 --> 00:09:11,547
The particular weakness at Barings
Singapore, one person running

143
00:09:11,627 --> 00:09:16,200
both the trading and the settlement,
is now ruled out in writing.

144
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:20,219
The Monetary Authority of
Singapore's guidelines on internal

145
00:09:20,299 --> 00:09:24,727
controls say a firm should have
processes that stop any one member

146
00:09:24,807 --> 00:09:29,236
of staff from handling an entire
transaction from start to finish,

147
00:09:29,316 --> 00:09:33,198
and they name trade execution
combined with settlement and

148
00:09:33,278 --> 00:09:37,650
reconciliation as an example of
inadequate separation of duties.

149
00:09:37,650 --> 00:09:41,225
Rogue trading has not
disappeared, however.

150
00:09:41,225 --> 00:09:46,074
In January 2008 the French
bank Société Générale announced

151
00:09:46,154 --> 00:09:51,682
losses of €4.9 billion from
unauthorised trades by Jérôme Kerviel,

152
00:09:51,762 --> 00:09:56,186
and in September 2011 the
Swiss bank UBS lost about 2

153
00:09:56,266 --> 00:10:01,450
billion US dollars through trades
by Kweku Adoboli in London.

154
00:10:01,450 --> 00:10:05,345
Both banks survived, but in
both cases the positions had

155
00:10:05,425 --> 00:10:09,400
grown very large before the
bank's controls caught them.

156
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:13,263
The 1914 run on a Singapore
bank showed how quickly

157
00:10:13,343 --> 00:10:17,284
confidence can leave a bank;
Barings showed how much

158
00:10:17,364 --> 00:10:21,075
damage one unchecked desk
can do from inside it.

159
00:10:21,075 --> 00:10:24,903
Where it fits in the bigger story:
Barings is usually remembered

160
00:10:24,983 --> 00:10:28,872
as the bank that one man broke,
but the controls that should have

161
00:10:28,952 --> 00:10:32,596
stopped him were missing on a
trading floor at Raffles Place,

162
00:10:32,676 --> 00:10:36,748
and Singapore's own inspectors and
lawmakers did much of the work of

163
00:10:36,828 --> 00:10:40,675
explaining what went wrong and
tightening the rules afterwards.

164
00:10:40,675 --> 00:10:43,866
The most useful lesson
from it is a simple one:

165
00:10:43,946 --> 00:10:47,485
a trader whose profits look
too good to be true is a

166
00:10:47,565 --> 00:10:50,975
reason to look harder,
not a reason to look away.
